speechtext:
Right	Hon.	J.	G.	Diefenbaker	(Prime
Minister): Mr. Speaker, having regard to the fact that this is indeed the Queen's day, as I said earlier, and in the light of the moving experience which has been ours during the last few minutes, 1 could not but think of the words of the poet John Masefield in which he described the Queen in this way: An order and a beauty from of old Set by its virtue above greed and hate;
A loneliness of living crowned with gold;
In all life's storm a standard of the state.
Without further words I feel that all hon. members in this house would wish to join in the motion to be moved by myself and seconded by the Right Hon. Mr. St. Laurent in these words:
That a humble address be presented to Her Majesty conveying the loyalty and love of this house and of all her Canadian subjects, their joy in welcoming the presence in Canada of Her Majesty and His Royal Highness The Prince Philip, and their profound gratitude to Her Majesty for graciously opening parliament and making of this day an historic occasion for all Canadians.
